From: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Subject: [PATCH] modesetting: draw a screen of one bit a pixel

The driver took depths 15, 16, 24 and 30; the server's fb code draws one
bit a pixel, and a monochrome LCD's DRM driver offers DRM_FORMAT_D1.
Depth 1 is now drawn in the shadow and copied to a dumb buffer of a bit a
pixel through a table: Linux keeps the leftmost pixel in a byte's most
significant bit where X on a little-endian machine keeps it in the least,
and D1 calls 1 dark where X's StaticGray calls it white.  The framebuffer
is added as D1, since depth and bits a pixel of 1 name the indexed C1; a
row's width in pixels is the pitch's bits over the bits a pixel, not its
bytes over the bytes; the shadow is a bit a pixel; bitmaps are in fb's
order, which xf86SetDepthBpp() would give the MSBFirst of VGA planes; and
there is no palette or gamma ramp.  A driver that prefers depth 1 gets it.
Without a dirty handler on D1, as a driver scanning the buffer in place
has none, updates cost no ioctl.

Signed-off-by: Tim Douglas <me@timdoug.com>
Upstream: N/A a one-bit panel
---
